  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Strong to severe storms will be possible across most \nof the area through Friday, with isolated flash flooding possible in \nthe western mountains. \n\nSubsequent upper-level shortwave troughs will traverse east across \nthe Mid-Atlantic over the next few days. Winds become more westerly, \nallowing a leeside trough to form across the Piedmont. This \ncombination will allow for some strong to severe storms to form each \nafternoon with the aid of diurnal heating. A Bermuda high in the \nWestern Atlantic will also continue to feed a southerly flow of \nwarm, moist air into the area, especially by tomorrow, further \nenhancing storm development. \n\nFor the rest of today, showers and storms have moved into the \nPiedmont and will continue to push south and east. Isolated severe \nweather remains possible, in the form of damaging wind gusts and \nsmall hail. Excessive rainfall also remains possible, generally \nanywhere south of I-64, which could cause isolated flash flooding in \nflood-prone areas. High rain rates of 2-4\"+ per hour, and any storms \nthat move over the same areas repeatedly would be the most likely \ncause of flooding in vulnerable locations. Showers/storms move out \nby this evening, with mostly quieter weather into the overnight \nhours. \n\nOn Thursday, moisture continues to increase, with precipitable water \namounts nearing 2.00\". This will allow storms to produce efficient \nrainfall and high rain rates, with isolated flash flooding possible, \nmainly confined to West Virginia. Severe weather will also be a \nthreat again tomorrow, as warmer temperatures and clearer skies will \nincrease instability. There is some uncertainty with storm coverage \nfor tomorrow, as CAM models have a wide range of possibilities. \nHowever, virtually all models have struggled to effectively capture \nstorm complexes over the past couple of days. Any storms that do \nform will be capable of damaging wind gusts and large hail. The \nentire area is in a Marginal Risk, with a Slight Risk for locations \nnorth of US-460. \n\nFriday will see yet another round of showers/storms in the \nafternoon, with the same areas in a Marginal and Slight Risk for \nsevere weather as was the case on Thursday. Damaging winds and \npossibly large hail will again be the threats. Isolated flash \nflooding is also possible in the far western mountains. The one \ndifference for Friday will be that the trough pushes east and a cold \nfront moves into the area, which will be the main forcing for \nshowers/storms to develop, leading to more widespread coverage. \n\nKey Message 2: Above normal temperatures Thursday through\nSunday.\n\nWinds look to turn westerly/southwesterly Thursday and Friday \nahead of an approaching cold front, that looks to push into the \nregion late in the day on Friday. Temperatures will be quite \nwarm with some downsloping winds leading to adiabatic warming \nboth Thursday and Friday east of the Blue Ridge in the Piedmont.\nDaytime highs each day look to climb into the mid 90s, with \nsome localized areas possibly reaching the mid to upper 90s \nacross the Piedmont. These high temperatures combined with \ndewpoint temperatures in the upper 60s to near 70 degrees across\nthe region will lead to Heat Index values in the 100-105 range \nacross much of the Piedmont on Thursday and Friday. These Heat \nIndex values are just below heat headlines for the Piedmont of \nVirginia and North Carolina; nevertheless, folks should still \nconsider taking frequent breaks indoors or the shade, as well as\ndrinking plenty of fluids if working outdoors during the peak \nof the daytime heat. \n\nWhile a frontal passage is expected Friday, temperatures do not\nlook to decrease on Saturday or Sunday; however, dewpoint\ntemperatures will decrease significantly enough that Heat index\nvalues will be tied closer to actual temperatures for the area.\nThe lower dewpoints will scour the mugginess across the area,\nwhich will inadvertently provide some relief from the heat.",
